  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how lithium metal and a solid electrolyte interact mechanically and electrochemically during cycling, and why different interphases can lead to stable or unstable behavior.\n\nAt the top, two schematic comparisons show how the microstructure and mechanical properties of the lithium layer and the solid electrolyte influence current distribution. During plating, current becomes constricted at contact points, driving filament or dendrite growth down into grain boundaries or weak regions of the solid electrolyte. During stripping, the reverse process leaves pores and voids at the interface, indicating loss of contact and uneven current flow. The message is that both materials’ microstructures, together with confinement and local current focusing, control failure.\n\nThe lower part contrasts three interfacial states. In the “Stable” case, lithium ions move uniformly across a clean interface while electrons stay on the metal side, so deposition remains even. In the “MCI” case, a mixed-conducting interphase appears to transport both ions and electrons, enabling internal reduction reactions and less controlled growth within the interphase. In the “SEI” case, the interphase seems ion-conductive but electronically insulating, blocking electron penetration and suppressing parasitic reduction. Overall, the figure links interphase transport properties and mechanics to dendrites, voids, and stability.",
